And that's the idea for these kind of&nbsp;
DFS traversals that we're dealing with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:09.440 --> 01:41:14.320
This brings us to our last section of algorithms&nbsp;
that we'll be looking at. And we'll look at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:14.320 --> 01:41:19.520
simple example with graphs. And we'll just see&nbsp;
how similar working with graphs is to something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:19.520 --> 01:41:24.080
like trees. And we're going to look at a very&nbsp;
popular algorithm known as depth first search.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:25.120 --> 01:41:30.160
And the idea is that we start at a node like a,&nbsp;
and we basically say we're searching for a node,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:30.160 --> 01:41:34.800
let's say the node we're searching for is H.&nbsp;
And so I start from a and I say, Okay, let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:34.800 --> 01:41:40.640
get all the neighbors and see is this my value,&nbsp;
so a is not my value. So I go to my neighbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:40.640 --> 01:41:48.320
and I say B, B is not my value, go to C, D is not&nbsp;
my value, but I get all my neighbors. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:48.320 --> 01:41:54.000
at an interesting point, because I'm searching&nbsp;
for H, right, which is in the far half. But when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:54.000 --> 01:41:59.760
I get my neighbor's, let's say the first neighbor&nbsp;
I get is E. And so I actually have to explore down&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:59.760 --> 01:42:06.480
the depths of E before I even consider going to H.&nbsp;
And so I go down, and I see E. And now I go to F.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:07.760 --> 01:42:11.520
And now I have to look at all of F's&nbsp;
neighbors. So the first neighbor I go to is K.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:12.720 --> 01:42:15.760
And if k had neighbors, I would&nbsp;
explore all of those neighbors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:15.760 --> 01:42:23.840
But it doesn't, so I can just pop off. And now I&nbsp;
go to J and back to F. Now I go to I am back to F,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:24.400 --> 01:42:30.560
and I kind of recurse backup the call stack.&nbsp;
So now that I've explored all the neighbors of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:31.440 --> 01:42:37.280
right, I've explored one neighbor of D, I&nbsp;
only have one unvisited neighbor left for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:37.280 --> 01:42:41.840
me to explore. And it's g so I go to G and I&nbsp;
say is this the value that I'm looking for?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:42.560 --> 01:42:46.560
And it's not. So I look at the neighbors of G&nbsp;
and I say is this the value I'm looking for?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:46.560 --> 01:42:52.080
And it is. And this is the idea of depth first&nbsp;
search. And so we're going to look at a piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:52.080 --> 01:42:55.840
of code and walk through it line by line and see&nbsp;
how something like this would work recursively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:58.960 --> 01:43:01.760
We look at a piece of code like&nbsp;
this, and we have the input node.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:02.880 --> 01:43:09.120
And to avoid cycles, which can happen in graphs,&nbsp;
which is different than trees will keep a set&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:09.120 --> 01:43:14.720
of visited values. So we never want to visit the&nbsp;
same node more than once. And so that's what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:14.720 --> 01:43:19.920
visited set is going to do. And the goal integer&nbsp;
here is just the node that we're looking for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:20.560 --> 01:43:25.200
So I know the graph we were just looking at was&nbsp;
letters, but we're going to assume that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:25.200 --> 01:43:33.520
working with a graph with integer values. And we&nbsp;
ask our base case questions again. So think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:33.520 --> 01:43:39.520
the smallest thing you could pass in if you pass&nbsp;
in an empty node, a normal node, then there's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:39.520 --> 01:43:45.280
way you could ever find the goal, because the&nbsp;
goal is an integer value. And so here, we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:45.280 --> 01:43:51.040
just return false. So this is a base case. The&nbsp;
other base case is if we've actually found the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:51.040 --> 01:43:57.360
node. So let's assume the node you pass in is the&nbsp;
gold node, then here we found the node. And so we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:57.360 --> 01:44:01.040
would just return true here, and this would imply&nbsp;
that we found the value that we're looking for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:02.960 --> 01:44:07.200
But let's assume that we haven't found the&nbsp;
value, just like the example. The first thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:07.200 --> 01:44:12.400
that we need to do is we need to aggregate all&nbsp;
of the neighbors from a given node. And so we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:12.400 --> 01:44:17.360
going to assume the node API has a function call&nbsp;
that allows you to aggregate all the neighbors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:19.040 --> 01:44:23.440
And the first question we ask is have&nbsp;
we have we visited this node before?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:24.160 --> 01:44:28.880
Remember, we want to avoid cycles. And so we&nbsp;
never want to visit a node more than once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:28.880 --> 01:44:34.400
And so if it's been visited, we just ignore it&nbsp;
and we continue. But if it hasn't been visited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:34.400 --> 01:44:39.840
then we add it to the visited set. And we start&nbsp;
the exploration. And we ask this question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:40.880 --> 01:44:46.560
have we found the node that we've been working&nbsp;
with with this particular neighbor. If we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:46.560 --> 01:44:54.160
found the solution, then we can just return&nbsp;
right away. Now if we haven't found the solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:54.160 --> 01:44:59.040
from this particular neighbor, then we'll continue&nbsp;
recursing through the rest of the neighbors&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:00.800 --> 01:45:06.720
And it's at that point, if we found it, we would&nbsp;
return true. And this brings us to our last base&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:06.720 --> 01:45:12.480
case, let's assume that we've traversed all of&nbsp;
the neighbors. And we never found the solution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:13.200 --> 01:45:18.320
That would mean that the goal value that we're&nbsp;
looking for was just never in the graph to begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:18.320 --> 01:45:23.760
with. And this brings us to our final base case&nbsp;
of just returning false. And this is a way to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:23.760 --> 01:45:29.200
hey, I've searched for this node, but it&nbsp;
didn't exist in the graph. It's false,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:29.200 --> 01:45:33.280
it did not show up in the search. And&nbsp;
that's where we can terminate the algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:36.480 --> 01:45:40.560
I think it's important to spend just a little&nbsp;
bit of time talking about optimizations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:40.560 --> 01:45:44.640
you can make when using recursion. And&nbsp;
the first that we're going to look at is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:44.640 --> 01:45:49.120
memoir, zation. And caching, which we&nbsp;
talked about briefly when writing Fibonacci.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:49.680 --> 01:45:54.560
And the question that we're trying to answer here&nbsp;
is how can we speed up our program by pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:54.560 --> 01:46:00.400
storing things that we've already re computed or&nbsp;
computed for the first time. So if I've computed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:00.400 --> 01:46:06.000
some very expensive operation, and I have to re&nbsp;
compute it again, in a subsequent recursive call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:06.000 --> 01:46:09.520
I want to check to see if I've done&nbsp;
that work already. And if I have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:09.520 --> 01:46:13.280
I'm just going to return the result instead&nbsp;
of re computing that operation again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:14.320 --> 01:46:18.800
And the best way to kind of conceptualize This&nbsp;
is looking at the Fibonacci tree sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:19.840 --> 01:46:24.800
Here, we see that I'm doing F of three twice,&nbsp;
and I'm re computing F of two, three times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:24.800 --> 01:46:30.160
And the question is, why can't I just compute&nbsp;
f of two once, and then just reuse that value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:30.160 --> 01:46:34.560
so I don't grow these sub trees. And it&nbsp;
turns out, you can do that really easily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:35.760 --> 01:46:41.520
Let's look at the modified Fibonacci code. Notice&nbsp;
I have a hashmap here and hashmaps have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:41.520 --> 01:46:48.640
property, such that we can retrieve values from&nbsp;
the map in constant time access, so big O of one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:48.640 --> 01:46:52.800
And the reason is because the data that we&nbsp;
put into the hashmap is hashed. And so we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:52.800 --> 01:46:58.720
constant time access to the memory addresses&nbsp;
in which that data is stored. And so when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:58.720 --> 01:47:03.920
call the Fibonacci function, the first thing&nbsp;
I always do is I say, Hey, does the cache have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:03.920 --> 01:47:10.000
this value. And you can also notice that I've pre&nbsp;
populated the cache with the base cases as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:12.000 --> 01:47:18.080
Now in the instance, that the cache does not have&nbsp;
the value, I still go about the recursive call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:18.080 --> 01:47:23.680
as I had done previously. But I ensure that&nbsp;
once that recursive call has been evaluated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:23.680 --> 01:47:27.600
I put the result into the cache.&nbsp;
And then I return my result.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:28.240 --> 01:47:33.440
And this is a really key component because&nbsp;
the cache is global, right. And so all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:33.440 --> 01:47:39.280
subsequent recursive calls are putting their&nbsp;
results in the cache. And as things get added,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:39.280 --> 01:47:43.760
and popped off the stack, I can just keep&nbsp;
cross referencing the cache to see if I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:43.760 --> 01:47:49.680
doing redundant work or not. And that saves me&nbsp;
a lot of computational power. So that's the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:49.680 --> 01:47:55.280
of memorization and caching. And you can do a&nbsp;
lot of this in a lot of different situations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:59.360 --> 01:48:06.000
The next optimization I want to just discuss is&nbsp;
this idea of tail call recursion optimization. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:06.000 --> 01:48:10.160
people usually refer to this as you know,&nbsp;
tail call optimization, or tail recursion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:11.120 --> 01:48:16.560
And the idea here is that basically, the compiler&nbsp;
in certain languages, especially functional&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:16.560 --> 01:48:22.480
programming languages, will optimize the number of&nbsp;
stack frames that get added to the call stack to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:23.040 --> 01:48:26.960
basically remove this idea of stack&nbsp;
overflows in a lot of scenarios.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:28.880 --> 01:48:34.320
Now, the way that the compiler does this analysis&nbsp;
is it really looks at the last function call.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:34.320 --> 01:48:40.640
And it has to be a recursive call. And we'll&nbsp;
look at an idea of comparing these two things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:41.760 --> 01:48:46.960
And there was actually a response on the&nbsp;
computer science Stack Exchange by this user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:46.960 --> 01:48:52.800
and he gave two examples. The first is&nbsp;
this simple recursive factorial function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:53.600 --> 01:49:00.560
And notice that the final return value is a&nbsp;
value multiplied by a recursive call. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:00.560 --> 01:49:08.080
this is not tail recursive, because the return&nbsp;
value is not just the recursive call. And so in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:08.080 --> 01:49:13.840
order for this to be tail recursive optimized,&nbsp;
we need to modify to look something like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:15.120 --> 01:49:18.960
Now, if you spend a second to look at this&nbsp;
code, you'll realize that functionally it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:18.960 --> 01:49:24.560
doing the exact same thing. But we've had&nbsp;
to modify the parameters to get to a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:24.560 --> 01:49:31.280
where we can build a function that's tail call&nbsp;
optimized. And the reason it's optimized with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:31.280 --> 01:49:37.280
tail call recursive property is because the final&nbsp;
return value is in itself just a recursive call.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:38.880 --> 01:49:45.280
And it turns out in certain operating in certain&nbsp;
languages, in their compilers, they've implemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:45.280 --> 01:49:51.040
some optimization techniques to exploit&nbsp;
this property and reduce stack overflows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:52.240 --> 01:49:56.480
Now, I just want to make a couple notes&nbsp;
on this. The rule of thumb is to always&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:56.480 --> 01:50:03.360
make your recursive calls be the last instruction&nbsp;
Nothing gets added to it, just the recursive call.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:04.800 --> 01:50:10.800
And the other thing to consider is that this is&nbsp;
supported mostly by functional languages. But it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:10.800 --> 01:50:19.440
not inherent in languages like Python and Java and&nbsp;
even JavaScript. There are certain browsers for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:19.440 --> 01:50:25.680
ies 2016. They do support tail call recursion&nbsp;
optimization for things like JavaScript,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:25.680 --> 01:50:31.360
but it's not supported across the board. And so&nbsp;
as you're considering this optimization technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:32.080 --> 01:50:36.960
think about what language you're using. And think&nbsp;
about, you know, the compiler that you're using&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:36.960 --> 01:50:42.080
and where your code is being executed to see if&nbsp;
this sort of optimization is supported or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

